[Applause]  dear prime minister  justin trudeau  the deputy prime minister christian  freeland  the minister of foreign affairs melanie  jolie  here ambassador  ambassador to leon  code  here ambassador hoskin  here mrs donselman of allianz who has  made it possible for us to host this  event  at the airlines forum at the brandenburg  gates thank you very much for  giving us the opportunity to meet here  in this building  esteemed members of the diplomatic corps  distinguished guests  canada and germany  are closely linked of course  in nato in which canada together  with all other allies protected the  freedom of west berlin  and west germany for decades  and together with others made  the peaceful reunification of our  country possible  it's a special honor for atlantic  brooker  and unit security conference to welcome  you  here justin trudeau  is our best guest of honor today to talk  about the situation in europe  atlantic worker turned 70 this year  and we are pleased that the pillars  of that bridge are built on a firm and  stable foundation  on both sides of the atlantic and i'm  especially proud that on this occasion i  can announce  that we have a founding of an atlantic  canada  as an independent  sister organization  ladies and gentlemen hardly any other  country is as closely linked to the  european ideas  of the enlightenment and its ideas of  freedom  democracy and human rights as canada is  and occasionally i thought sometimes  canada  is more european than some of our  european member states  therefore please allow me just  because my former colleague christian  freeland  is among us it would be a good signal  just now  if after more than five years  the german bundestag would finally  rectify  the free trade agreement sita  which not only brings advantages for  both sides  but above all is the most modern free  trade agreement of our time  because right now we are realizing  we are living in a time in which even  fundamental foundations  of peaceful coexistence between  peoples do not endure  war is once again  raging in the middle of europe for the  first time since 1939 when my country  started the second world war a war of  aggression  is being waged on europe soil again  almost 80 years  after the inhuman nazi regime was  defeated  and 30 years after the end of the cold  war  putin's war of aggression makes clear  that we are entering again  new dark ages  no other buildings represent this  difficult water history of europe and  germany  better than the great blockade when we  just went through  throughout the cold war it was the  symbol  of a world divided  a world in which two are geological  blocks stood face to face  threatening mutually assured destruction  with nuclear weapons  but it also stands for the ability to  overcome the dark ages  it was before the grendenberg gate where  u.s president ronald reagan famously  demanded  tear down this wall mr gorbachev  in 1990 when the soviet union collapsed  and the iron curtain fell  rattenberg cape became a symbol for  peace  and freedom in europe  today germany stands firm as a  democratic  liberal country in the transatlantic  community of elders  200 years after its construction it  seemed that red brickgate  finally would deserve the name that was  originally  intended for it  peacegate  but we also that even in europe war is  possible again even though russia had  moved well over 150 000 troops to  ukraine's border  since last year  until 10 days ago  we did not want to believe that vladimir  putin would  dare to invade  its neighbor  or that wars of aggression could return  to europe  russia's president's war of aggression  is not about ukraine  but about reconstituting this country as  a great power especially in europe  he wants to determine the future and  faith of europe again  and it's not the old soviet union that  serves as his model for this  but  zarest russia  russia's president  thinks that the categories of the 18th  and 19th century  wages a war for the means of the 20th  century and believes that he can  determine the 21st century with it  the conflict with russia is not about a  war of the west  against russia  as russian propaganda likes to publicly  publicly proclaim  russia is waging a war against all  fundamental norms  of international law  if the world community allowed this to  happen here in europe  it would be a dangerous lesson for other  properties in the  world for this reason  we are grateful for all those who  condemn this war of aggression  and use their opportunities to stand by  ukraine  and increase the pressure on russia's  president  to finally stop his war of aggression  war is horrible  we feel for the worst innocent victims  and admire the brave  and courageous ukrainians standing  firmly against the aggression  but we also feel for those in russia and  belarus that oppose this war  and their authocratic regimes but who  cannot express their opinion without  having to fear for their freedom and  their life  but we must not lose faith in ourselves  and the ability of our liberal  and free societies to withstand the  anachronism of war and autocracy  there is a future  this is precisely why in this dawning  21st century  the trans-atlantic partnership is taking  on a new significance  we have shown before that the peace gate  can be built  if we stand together and never give up  or believe that a better world is  possible  we can overcome the darkness of war  and conflict again  what is clear however is that europe and  its member states especially germany  must assume a greater role in  maintaining europe's security and peace  i am glad that my government has now  taken both steps in this direction  and shown its resolve to address the  tectonic geopolitical shifts  stretching beyond europe and the euro  atlantic that we are witnessing  at the dawn of the 21st century  and i'm sure your prime minister  minister trudeau he will give us now  important guidance  on how we can further strengthen the  transatlantic community  and work together to secure  freedom  peace and prosperity in all parts of  europe thank you very much for coming  and for your commitment  [Applause]  thank you sigmar  for that warm introduction it's so great  to see you again we  really did work very well together  during the season negotiations and  you've always been a friend and an ally  to canada and to democracies around the  world  i'm looking forward to my conversation  with anna and with all of you  i want to thank atlantic  for the ending munich security  confidence for bringing us together  it's a real honor for me to address you  today  it's also very good to see ambassador  hoisin today christopher i will always  remember that during your final closing  speech at the u.n security council  you chose to take part of your time  to call once again for the freedom of  michael coverage and michael spavor two  canadians who were arbitrarily detained  in china  thanks in part to your advocacy and the  advocacy of friends and allies around  the world those two men are at home now  thank you  when i was preparing my notes for  today's events  i remember  speaking in 2017 at the saint matthews  day banquet hosted by  then-mayor of hamburg hola  sigmar was there too  he actually delivered remarks right  before me that night too  the world has changed in the intervening  five years  what i said then remains true today  canada and germany  have long been partners on the world  stage because we share a progressive  vision of the world we understand the  importance of international cooperation  and partnership  we value  the rule of law  and democracy  it's great to be back in berlin today  of all places the people of berlin  know what it is  to fight for democracy  i don't have to tell you how much work  it takes  because you already understand that  democracy never happens  by accident  and as we're reminded now  it certainly doesn't continue  without effort  when vladimir putin launched a  full-scale invasion of ukraine  he violated russia's international  treaties and he attacked the values that  form the pillars of all democracies  this kind of brazen disregard for law  and human life  is a massive threat to europe  and to the world  foreign  [Music]  foreign president zalensky and ukrainian  people who are demonstrating so much  courage  and resilience  they're not only defending their country  they're defending the democratic values  that are so important to all of us  they're standing up to authoritarianism  canada and germany  stand with them  with our allies and partners we've put  in place  the toughest sanctions ever imposed on a  major economy  we're making sure putin  is held accountable for his actions  canada also remains unwavering in our  support for nato  we're supporting nato's efforts to  protect euro-atlantic security  democracy  and the rules-based international system  we worked so hard to build together  last month  chancellor schultz made a historic  announcement to shift german policy  to meet the challenges of today  including by supporting ukraine with  legal aid and military equipment  while canada and germany have stepped up  to help defend ukraine  so has all of europe  under the leadership of president funda  a proud european and a proud german  the european union is mobilized to  defend democracy  and in this germany the european union  can count on the full  support of canada  vladimir putin is undoubtedly a threat  to ukrainians  and a threat to people beyond their  borders  but even as we are clear-eyed about the  challenge he represents  we mustn't despair  because at its best  democracy  is always  stronger than authoritarianism  but if we're going to be honest with  each other  democracy hasn't exactly been at its  best  these past few years  even as we're fighting putin's invasion  we need to recommit ourselves  to the work of strengthening our  democracies  apathy  cynicism  misinformation and disinformation  declining voter turnout  mistrust in our institutions  it's our job  to rebuild  that trust  to give people reasons to have faith  in our institutions  to get involved  in civic life  we can't let  fear anxiety  and envy  overcome the hope  the  earnest  optimism  that is  necessary  for democracies to thrive  we can't settle for simplistic answers  to complex problems  we can't let nativism  distract from the hard work of  building better systems and real  solutions  these challenges are not easy for any of  us  but addressing them head-on  and  [Music]  we all  have a responsibility to deal in facts  into flight to fight the flood of  disinformation and manipulation  while social media has a profound  positive effect on society it also has a  role in enabling harmful content like  hate speech and misinformation  domestically our government is working  on a new new legislation to address  online harms  this year  canada is chair of the freedom online  coalition  and our goal is to encourage the public  to have informed and meaningful  engagement in society both offline and  online  we all have more work to do  in the 1930s  thomas mann  described how democracy is built on the  respect  for the infinite dignity  of each individual  one of the most powerful ideas canada  hopes to share with the world is  our deep and abiding belief in diversity  not only do we believe in it  we embrace it  although that's not to say we have it  all figured out  we're making progress  but there's always more work to be done  on fighting systemic racism  on gender equality  on defending lgbtq2 communities  on reconciliation  indigenous peoples  last summer  mary simon became  the first indigenous governor general in  canada's history  when she was in germany for her first  state visit just a few months ago  she led canada's delegation  at the frankfurt book fair  because it's only through sharing  knowledge  and listening  to indigenous people's perspectives  that we can hope to fully achieve  reconciliation  that's why we need  to stay open to hearing their truths and  honoring their stories through books  oral history and celebrating together  their culture  the respect  for the infinite dignity of each  individual  means no one  should get left out  this is also true  for people who hold different political  views  we all need to commit  to more listening  and less shouting  diversity of ideas  helps us learn from one another  talking with people who think  differently from us  is how we challenge ourselves  challenging ourselves is how we grow  so by strengthening our open inclusive  societies  everyone benefits  democracy benefits  it is true for canada  it is true for germany  and it's true for everyone around the  world  whether it's  the immediate challenge of high gas  prices  or the long-term consequences of climate  change  democracies  need to be at their best  to have the tough conversations needed  to figure that out  earlier today  germany signed on to canada's global  carbon pricing challenge and canada  signed on to germany's climate  club now not only do we both have a  price on pollution at home we will both  be driving this work on the world stage  too  canada and germany are  also two of the world's biggest  economies  so when we stand united on something  it matters  after all as this crisis in ukraine has  laid bare  we must keep working together  i know that european leaders have been  calling for acceleration and the shift  to clean sustainable power as putin  tries to hold a continent of homes  hostage  over oil and gas  so i want to commend  the leadership of chancellor schultz to  halt  the certification of nordstrom 2 russian  pipeline  this was not an easy decision but it was  the right decision  you will not bend  to intimidation and you will find a path  forward on a secure and sustainable  energy future you've made your vision  clear  and canada will stand with you  my friends  as long as we don't take our democracy  for granted  as long as we keep working to make it  better  as long as we stay united  we needn't ever feel intimidated by  tyrants  over the past years  putin made the mistake of seeing  disagreements  as division  and debates  as weakness  it's actually our greatest strength  this friday  will mark two years since the world  health organization declared covet 19 a  pandemic  this health crisis has been a challenge  unlike any other  but it also showed  how people stepped up  for one another  everyone rolled up their sleeves  and worked together  that  is the story of the pandemic  we worked together  just like we're seeing now from people  all around the world offering to welcome  ukrainian refugees  and send humanitarian aid  putin  made serious miscalculations  he underestimated the incredible courage  of the ukrainian people  and he underestimated the resolve and  unity  of democratic allies and partners  in the face of authoritarianism  we have to continue fighting lies with  truth  fear with facts  division  with unity  as you all know  the atlantic  turns 70 years old this year  i can tell you that this bridge of  transatlantic cooperation between our  nations will remain strong and sturdy  for many years to come  like i said  a lot has changed since i was in humber  five years ago  however  the importance of working together to  allow democracy to flourish and  ultimately to prevail  is as true today  as it was then  democracy  is not just a concept  in a political science textbook  it's our everyday lives  standing up for our shared values  requires  constant efforts  but i know that canadians  germans and people everywhere are up to  the challenge  in the words of president zielenski  light  will win over darkness  [Applause]

